# AWS EC2 Hands-on

1. Opened EC2 Service

Opened the AWS Management Console and navigated to the EC2 service.

Opened the Instances section and started the EC2 launch process.

2. Selected Ubuntu AMI

Selected Ubuntu as the operating system for the EC2 instance.

AMI provides the template from which the EC2 instance is created.

Simple idea:

Ubuntu AMI
|
↓
EC2 Instance

3. Selected Instance Type

Selected the t3.micro instance type for practice.

Instance Type determines the computing capacity of the EC2 instance, including resources such as CPU and RAM.

Simple idea:

t3.micro → Small EC2 computing capacity

4. Created Key Pair

Created a new Key Pair for secure authentication.

Key Pair name:

ec2-ubuntu-day5

The private key file was downloaded in ".pem" format.

The ".pem" file must be kept secure and should never be uploaded to GitHub.

Simple idea:

Private Key → Secure SSH Login → Ubuntu EC2

5. Configured Security Group

Configured a Security Group for the EC2 instance.

Enabled:

- SSH traffic
- Source: My IP

This allows SSH access to the EC2 instance from my current IP address.

Simple idea:

My IP
|
Port 22 / SSH
|
Security Group
|
Ubuntu EC2

6. Configured Storage

Reviewed the default storage configuration.

The EC2 instance uses an EBS root volume for persistent storage.

Simple idea:

EC2 → EBS → Storage

7. Final EC2 Configuration

Before launching, verified the main configuration:

AMI → Ubuntu

Instance Type → t3.micro

Key Pair → ec2-ubuntu-day5

Security Group → SSH / My IP

Storage → Default EBS

Quick Revision

Ubuntu AMI → Operating system template

t3.micro → EC2 instance type

Key Pair → Secure authentication

.pem → Private SSH key file

